{"name":"Cold-pressed raw milk bath","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_%282413988302%29.jpg/960px-Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_%282413988302%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_(2413988302).jpg","caption":"Milk bath spa in DVN (2413988302)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A milk bath is a bath taken in milk instead of, or added to, water, often scented with honey, rose or essential oils. Fans say it leaves skin soft and bright.","The appearance logic is that milk's lactic acid, an alpha hydroxy acid, loosens the proteins holding dead skin cells together, giving a smoother surface. The practice is linked to Cleopatra and Poppaea in legend, and to queens such as Elizabeth I."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People add milk or milk powder, sometimes with flower petals or oils, to a warm bath and soak. Raw or cold-pressed milk versions are favoured by some for being unprocessed."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Milk baths are generally a topical soak; people with milk allergy or sensitive skin may react, and milk left in warm water can sour."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_%282413988302%29.jpg/960px-Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_%282413988302%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_%282413988302%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Milk bath spa in DVN (2413988302)","license":"CC BY 2.0","creator":"Dennis Wong from Hong Kong, Hong Kong","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milk_bath_spa_in_DVN_(2413988302).jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://upload.w