com/page/Milk_bath"},{"title":"Milk bath - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk_bath"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Skin","chain":["Soak in milk","Lactic acid loosens dead skin cells","Softer, smoother skin"],"effect":"claimed","text":"Soakers use milk baths for softer, brighter-looking skin, relying on the mild exfoliating effect of lactic acid."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Milk_bath","lead":["A milk bath is a bath in milk, often scented with honey, rose, daisies or essential oils, used for a soft, smooth complexion.[1]","The practice is tied in legend to Cleopatra bathing in donkey milk, though this is unconfirmed. Historian Adrian Goldsworthy believes Empress Poppaea set the fashion about 80 years after Cleopatra's death.[1] Queens Catherine Parr and Elizabeth I reportedly bathed in milk to look younger and paler.[1]","The article explains the claimed action through lactic acid, an alpha hydroxy acid that dissolves proteins holding dead skin cells together.[1] Commercial products include a Chinese patent for a milk bath salt and a donkey milk cosmetic claimed to moisturise and whiten.[2][3]"],"infobox":[["Type","Beauty bath"],["Active idea","Lactic acid (alpha hydroxy acid)"],["Legendary users","Cleopatra, Poppaea, Elizabeth I"],["Typical additions","Honey, rose petals, essential oils"]],"sections":[{"h":"History","paras":["Cleopatra became associated with milk baths mainly through Claudette Colbert's 1930s film portrayals of her and Poppaea. Anna Held was also reported to bathe in milk. Buttermilk baths were historically a grooming method for show animals.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Products","paras":["Patents describe a milk bath salt mixing milk with salt and fragrance for beauty and health care, and a donkey milk cosmetic with over 30 percent donkey milk positioned as moisturiser and whitening agent.[2][3]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[],"refs":[{"title":